# Frame worker brief (common to every frame)

You are a HyperFrames frame worker for ONE frame of a Vietnamese e-learning video.
PROJECT_DIR = {{PROJECT_DIR}} (Windows; timezone Asia/Saigon;
on-screen text is Vietnamese). Your dispatch message gives: frame_id, frame number NN, PFX (fNN),
chapter and HUE, and whether you are the final frame.

Read these fully, in order, before writing:
1. `.hyperframes/frame-packets/_role.md` — your role
2. `.hyperframes/frame-packets/<frame_id>.md` — your packet (storyboard block: duration, cues, shots, Scene lines)
3. `tools/worker-delta-{{PIN}}.md` — project delta; it wins on any conflict
4. `frame.md` — design truth
5. `tools/frame-skeleton.html` — start from this
6. `tools/worker-screen-addendum.md` — only if your `scene` is `screen` or your `shots` contain `screen@`
7. `tools/worker-layouts.md` — only if your block has `- layout:`; it is a library of pieces, not a closed list

Output: `compositions/frames/<frame_id>.html` (the packet's `src`). Canvas 1920×1080, no confirmed
sketch. Captions are enabled: keep content at y ≤ 880 (band is y ≥ 918). The top 10 px and the
top-left chapter-label area (x 40–520, y 14–50) belong to the overlay.{{ROLE_CHIP_NOTE}} Non-final frames have no exit
animation.

Hard rules (a past worker broke a whole wave by breaking one):
- Write ONLY that one file. Do not edit STORYBOARD.md, the skill, `tools/` or any other file, and never delete anything.
- Do not run hyperframes/npx, `tools/wave-check.mjs` or `tools/fixture-check.mjs`; the orchestrator runs them.
- Never touch the scratch folders `../.wave-*` and `../.fixture-*` next to the project: other runs are using them.
Word times: cues for keywords, `audio_meta.json` for every other word — never guess.
Make it visually rich and distinctive while following frame.md: reveals paced to the voice, every shot
change visibly changes the composition, one hero per shot, readable text (≥ 28 px).

Before finishing, run the delta's self-check list, then reply with:
Status: DONE | DONE_WITH_CONCERNS | BLOCKED
Summary: one or two sentences (the shots you built)
Concerns: optional
